write_timing_model runs one filtered arrival search per input pin and
transition. The search, its endpoints and the cleanup after it already
stay inside the input's fanout cone; findOutputDelays did not: it
walked every top-level pin and every path on every output port, so its
cost was the product of input and output port counts. Output ports are
endpoints, so the outputs the input reaches are among the filtered
endpoints the loop already collected; walk those instead.

On a generated block with 4 outputs per input (each input buffered
into a flop, outputs buffered from the flops, every 8th from an input)
write_timing_model drops from 8.1 s to 0.92 s at 4,096 inputs and from
42 s to 3.0 s at 8,192, the .lib byte-identical at every size, and
small blocks are no slower. This replaces parallaxsw/OpenSTA pull
request 478 here: the batching takes 1.9 s and 8.4 s at those sizes.
Where cones overlap densely (1,024 inputs, 8 XOR layers) the per-input
propagation dominates instead, this change leaves it at 13 s and the
batching halves it.

Upstream as parallaxsw/OpenSTA#535. Retires on a //:bump onto an
OpenROAD whose OpenSTA carries it.

